{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"uav_architecture_role_and_topology:1","status":"PARTIAL","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[2,3,4,6],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§II-A, §II-C, §IV-A","excerpt_or_cell":"The MDs are randomly distributed in a network area of 400m × 400m with UAV clusters including 4 UAVs as edge servers. The UAVs fly at a fixed altitude (i.e., H) of 100 meters. And flying speed of the UAVs is 10 m/s in a clockwise direction. ... The maximum covered ground radius of the UAV (i.e., R) is set to 200 meters. The BS is deployed at (200, 200) m.","value_as_printed":"4 UAVs; H=100 meters; speed 10 m/s clockwise; R=200 meters (coverage radius); 1 BS at (200, 200) m","units":"m, m/s","baseline":null,"workload":"DNN-based tasks from MDs","conditions":"400m x 400m area; initial UAV positions (150,150), (150,250), (250,150), (250,250) m; BS as fallback cloud server for reprocessing (§II-A p. 2, §II-C-2 p. 3)","rationale":"Supported for 4 UAVs at H=100 m acting as MEC edge servers, 10 m/s clockwise motion, and 1 BS at (200,200) m as fallback cloud server. PARTIAL because the clause '(circular flight at 10 m/s, radius R=200 m)' misattributes R=200 m as a flight radius; §IV-A (p. 6) and constraint C7 (p. 4) define R=200 m as the UAV's maximum covered ground radius, and 'circular flight' is not stated (only 'in a clockwise direction')."}
{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"inference_model_task_and_dataset:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[3,6,7],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§II-C, §IV-A, §IV-B, Fig. 3, Fig. 4","excerpt_or_cell":"By default, the classical DNN model, AlexNet [22] is used as the DNN-based task. ... We also select well-known DNN models, TinyYOLOv2 [23], NiN [24] and VGG16 [25] for comparison. ... Note that MDs will further process the classification/detection stage (i.e., fully connected layers) locally.","value_as_printed":"AlexNet (default), TinyYOLOv2, NiN, VGG16; conv/pool offloaded, FC local; dataset not named","units":null,"baseline":null,"workload":"AlexNet (default), TinyYOLOv2, NiN, VGG16","conditions":"Feature extraction (conv/pool layers 1..L_k) partitioned and offloaded; FC layers processed locally on MD; all pages 1-8 (§I-§V, References) inspected confirming no evaluation dataset is named","rationale":"§IV-A (p. 6) specifies AlexNet as default, §IV-B (p. 7) and Figs. 3-4 evaluate AlexNet, NiN, TinyYOLOv2, and VGG16, §II-C (p. 3) states conv/pool feature extraction is offloaded while FC layers run locally on MDs, and full-text inspection (pp. 1-8) confirms no dataset is named."}
{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"partition_and_assignment:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[2,3],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§II-A, §II-C, Fig. 2","excerpt_or_cell":"Assuming that the MD partitions the input data along the height. And if we determine which portions of the output rows at the last layer L_k that the UAV should send back to the MD, then, backtracking the corresponding input rows of all the previous layers by using the Arbitrary Input Range (AIR) algorithm [15], which allows each partitioned segment of the previous layers' regional workload to be treated as a separate task as shown in Fig. 2.","value_as_printed":"N_m subtasks; row-wise height partitioning via AIR backtracking across conv/pool layers 1..L_k","units":"rows, subtasks","baseline":null,"workload":"DNN feature-extraction layers (conv/pool)","conditions":"Output rows [h^0_mi(t), h^1_mi(t)] at layer L_k are disjoint (C5) and sum to H^out_{L_k} (C6); overlapped input rows backtracked via AIR; each subtask offloaded to at most one UAV (C2)","rationale":"§II-A (p. 2) defines partitioning each MD task J_m(t) into N_m subtasks offloaded to UAVs, and §II-C with Fig. 2 (p. 3) confirms row-wise partitioning along height using AIR backtracking across conv/pool layers into independent subtasks."}
{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[6],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§III-C","excerpt_or_cell":"Then, we propose the LaCPOO algorithm to solve the dual problem in (26). The algorithm iteratively combines subgradient projection with the CPOO method to find feasible solutions. During each iteration, Lagrange multipliers are updated using a fixed step size, while transmission powers are adjusted according to the KKT conditions.","value_as_printed":"LaCPOO = subgradient projection + CPOO; transmission powers updated via KKT conditions (Eq. 30)","units":null,"baseline":null,"workload":"Master problem Eq. (22) / Lagrange dual Eq. (26)","conditions":"Iterative Lagrangian dual updates with fixed step size (Eqs. 31-36) and inner KKT optimality (Eqs. 27-30)","rationale":"§III-C (p. 6) explicitly states that LaCPOO iteratively combines subgradient projection with CPOO and adjusts transmission powers according to KKT conditions."}
{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"compute_and_radio:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[2,3,6],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§II-B, §II-C-2, §IV-A","excerpt_or_cell":"The bandwidth b_0 and b_B is set to 10 MHz and 15 MHz, respectively. The background noise N_0 is set to -100 dBm [19]. The maximum transmission power of MD is 2 mW, and the maximum computation capacities of the MDs, the UAV are set to 1, 4-8 GHz, respectively. The processing density is set to 8 double-precision FLOPS/cycle [20]. ... The reliability threshold is set to 0.9, and the channel fading is assumed to follow the distribution with mean of 2 and variance of 0.4 [21].","value_as_printed":"MD 1 GHz; UAV 4-8 GHz; 8 double-precision FLOPS/cycle; p_m^max=2 mW; b_0=10 MHz; b_B=15 MHz; N_0=-100 dBm; fading mean=2, variance=0.4; reliability threshold=0.9","units":"GHz, double-precision FLOPS/cycle, mW, MHz, dBm","baseline":null,"workload":"Default simulation setup (AlexNet)","conditions":"Orthogonal G2U channels (b_0, §II-B p. 2) and G2B fallback channel (b_B, §II-C-2 p. 3); default simulation parameters in §IV-A (p. 6)","rationale":"Every parameter value and unit matches §IV-A (p. 6) verbatim, with b_0 and b_B defined as G2U and G2B bandwidths on pp. 2-3."}
{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[3],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§II-C-2","excerpt_or_cell":"Due to the unreliable channel condition between the UAV and the MD, there may be a situation that the MD m can not receive the return data from all the UAVs, then, the MD will send the whole task to the BS for reprocessing. ... the MD transmits to the BS with the maximum power (i.e., p_m^max).","value_as_printed":"Whole task resent to BS using p_m^max","units":null,"baseline":null,"workload":"Full input task d_1^in","conditions":"When MD m cannot receive return data from all offloading UAVs (Lambda_m(t) != Lambda_m^S(t), Eq. 14 p. 4)","rationale":"§II-C-2 (p. 3) directly states that when an MD fails to receive return data from all UAVs under unreliable channels, it sends the whole task to the BS for reprocessing at maximum transmission power p_m^max."}
{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"evaluation_platform_simulator_hardware_and_traces:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[6],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§IV-A","excerpt_or_cell":"The MDs are randomly distributed in a network area of 400m × 400m with UAV clusters including 4 UAVs as edge servers. The UAVs fly at a fixed altitude (i.e., H) of 100 meters. And flying speed of the UAVs is 10 m/s in a clockwise direction. ... The BS is deployed at (200, 200) m.","value_as_printed":"400m x 400m square area; 4 clockwise-moving UAVs at H=100 m; BS at (200, 200) m","units":"m, m/s","baseline":null,"workload":"Default simulation scenario","conditions":"Randomly distributed MDs; UAV initial positions (150,150), (150,250), (250,150), (250,250) m; simulator software/hardware not reported","rationale":"§IV-A (p. 6) confirms all geometric and deployment elements in the assertion, with (200, 200) m at the centre of the 400m x 400m square area."}
{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"baselines_and_workloads:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[7],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§IV-A, Fig. 3, Fig. 5, Fig. 7","excerpt_or_cell":"For performance comparison, we implement the following three baselines: • Local: It processes all DNN inferences locally. • RPOM: It partitions the last convolutional layer randomly and offloads the subtask randomly. • Edgent-P [10]: Each MD selects the nearest UAV, and then divides the DNN-based task into two parts based on computing resources.","value_as_printed":"Local, RPOM, Edgent-P","units":null,"baseline":"Local, RPOM, Edgent-P (plus authors' CPOO vs. LaCPOO)","workload":"AlexNet, NiN, TinyYOLOv2, VGG16","conditions":"Evaluated in §IV-B Figs. 3-7 (note: RPOM is typoed as 'PROM' in §IV-B prose on p. 7)","rationale":"§IV-A (p. 7) explicitly lists Local, RPOM, and Edgent-P as the three implemented baselines."}
{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"sensitivity_and_variability:1","status":"PARTIAL","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[7],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§IV-B, Fig. 5","excerpt_or_cell":"Fig. 5 depicts the average delay under different bandwidth. As shown in the figure, all the algorithms progressively reduce the average delay as the transmission bandwidth increases.","value_as_printed":"Bandwidth sweep 10-30 MHz; Edgent-P, RPOM, CPOO, LaCPOO decrease; Local constant at ~980 ms (Fig. 5)","units":"MHz, ms","baseline":"Local, Edgent-P, RPOM, CPOO, LaCPOO","workload":"Default DNN model (AlexNet)","conditions":"Bandwidth varied from 10 to 30 MHz in 5 MHz steps (Fig. 5)","rationale":"PARTIAL: Although §IV-B prose (p. 7) states 'all the algorithms progressively reduce the average delay as the transmission bandwidth increases', Fig. 5 (p. 7) directly contradicts the clause 'all compared algorithms reduce average inference delay' because the Local baseline remains completely flat at ~980 ms across 10-30 MHz; only the four offloading algorithms (RPOM, Edgent-P, CPOO, LaCPOO) reduce delay."}
{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"code_and_data:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8],"section_or_table_or_figure":"All sections (§I-§V, footnotes, References)","excerpt_or_cell":null,"value_as_printed":"Not reported","units":null,"baseline":null,"workload":null,"conditions":"Inspected physical pp. 1-8 (Title/Abstract, §I Introduction, §II System Model, §III Solution, §IV Simulation Results, §V Conclusion, References)","rationale":"Explicit absence verified: no code repository, software link, dataset URL, or data availability statement appears anywhere in pp. 1-8."}
