{"report_id":"R0044","evidence_key":"reported_limitations:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0044.pdf","physical_pages":[3,6,7,8],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§II-C (p. 3), §IV (pp. 6-8), §V Conclusion (p. 8)","excerpt_or_cell":null,"value_as_printed":"Not reported","units":null,"baseline":null,"workload":null,"conditions":"Inspected physical pp. 1-8, specifically §V Conclusion (p. 8) and §IV Simulation Results (pp. 6-8)","rationale":"Explicit absence verified: §V Conclusion (p. 8) contains no limitations or future-work discussion; only routine modeling simplifications appear in §II-C (p. 3, e.g., ignoring UAV-to-MD return latency, BS compute/return delay, and G2B power optimization)."}
{"report_id":"R0048","evidence_key":"uav_architecture_role_and_topology:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","physical_pages":[2,4,8,9,10,11],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§I, §III-C, §VI-B, §VII-A-2, Table II, §VII-B","excerpt_or_cell":"The JDTSO algorithm achieved the lowest inference latency when the UAV's number is 20 or fewer. ... However, the MAPDP algorithm can provide better solutions for larger UAV scales. This supports the architecture UCAA we designed for UAV collaborative inference system: JDTSO algorithm is suitable for small-scale UAVs or setups with a central BS, while MAPDP algorithm is preferable for large-scale UAVs or setups lacking a BS.","value_as_printed":"UCAA; V=5, 10, 20, 40, 60, 100, 200 (and 80 in Figs. 3-8); h=200m; 500m x 500m area; JDTSO <=20 UAVs; MAPDP (Dec-POMDP) >20 UAVs","units":"UAVs, m","baseline":null,"workload":"Collaborative real-time video analytics","conditions":"Surveillance area 500m x 500m (§VII-A-2 p. 9); constant hovering altitude h=200m (Table II p. 9); distributed Dec-POMDP formulation (§VI-B p. 8)","rationale":"All elements are directly supported across §I (p. 2), §III-C (p. 4), §VI-B (p. 8), §VII-A-2 & Table II (p. 9), and §VII-B (pp. 10-11)."}
{"report_id":"R0048","evidence_key":"inference_model_task_and_dataset:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","physical_pages":[3,9],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§III-A-1, Fig. 1, §VII-A-1","excerpt_or_cell":"For the inference engine, we use an efficient YOLOv3-tiny model for each classifier [41]. ... Each UAV collects sensor data to monitor targets and generates ROIs using its onboard background subtractor. The tasks are the extracted ROIs that contain vehicles or persons from a video camera frame every 10 frames.","value_as_printed":"Real-time video analytics (vehicle/person attribute recognition); background subtractor ROI every 10 frames; YOLOv3-tiny classifiers in a 2-branch DAG","units":"frames","baseline":null,"workload":"Extracted vehicle/person ROIs every 10 frames","conditions":"OpenCV 3.2.0 background subtractor; branching DAG (Vehicle: Color->Type->Make; Person: Gender->Behavior, Fig. 1 p. 3); no named public video dataset","rationale":"§III-A-1 and Fig. 1 (p. 3) confirm vehicle/person attribute recognition via background subtractor ROI extraction and a branching classifier DAG, and §VII-A-1 (p. 9) confirms YOLOv3-tiny per classifier and ROI extraction every 10 frames."}
{"report_id":"R0048","evidence_key":"partition_and_assignment:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","physical_pages":[3,5,7,8],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§II-C, §IV-B, §V-B, Algorithm 1, §VI-B, Algorithm 2","excerpt_or_cell":"This paper focuses on partitioning classifiers at the classifier level. ... The DAG partition points C for each UAV are determined via brute-force enumeration based on the maximization objective in Equation (16) (line 15).","value_as_printed":"Classifier-level DAG partitioning (enumerated partition points C); binary offloading U_{v,i}^{v'}","units":null,"baseline":null,"workload":"Branching attribute-recognition DAG","conditions":"Brute-force enumeration of C (with max enumeration limit R for large scale, §V-C p. 7 & §VI-C p. 9); U_{v,i}^{v'} in {0,1} offloads task i of UAV v to neighboring UAV v' (§IV-A/B p. 5)","rationale":"§II-C (p. 3) specifies classifier-level partitioning, §IV-B (p. 5) defines U_{v,i}^{v'} for offloading to neighboring UAVs, and §V-B (p. 7) / §VI-B (p. 8) describe enumerating DAG partition points C."}
{"report_id":"R0048","evidence_key":"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","physical_pages":[2,6,7,8,12],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§I, §V-B, Algorithm 1, §VI-B, Algorithm 2, §VIII","excerpt_or_cell":"At the upper layer optimization, a genetic algorithm (GA) is presented to determine UAV positions. At the lower layer optimization, dynamic programming (DP) and a DAG partition strategy are used to obtain the exact-optimal solution. ... The proposed algorithm, MAPDP, combines multi-agent proximal policy optimization (MAPPO) with a DAG partition strategy.","value_as_printed":"JDTSO: GA (positioning) + DP (offloading) + convex optimization (bandwidth) + DAG partitioning; MAPDP: MAPPO + DAG partitioning","units":null,"baseline":null,"workload":"Centralized MINLP P1 (p. 6) and distributed Dec-POMDP P5 (pp. 7-8)","conditions":"Note: §V-B (pp. 6-7) and §VIII (p. 12) additionally specify a convex optimization tool (CVXPY) in JDTSO's lower layer for communication resource allocation","rationale":"Directly matches §I (p. 2) for both JDTSO and MAPDP; §V-B (pp. 6-7) and §VIII (p. 12) further detail convex optimization for bandwidth allocation in JDTSO's lower layer."}
{"report_id":"R0048","evidence_key":"compute_and_radio:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","physical_pages":[5,9],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§IV-B, §VII-A-2, Table II","excerpt_or_cell":"We use two types of UAVs, each equipped with a Raspberry Pi 3 B+ and an Intel Movidius Neural Computing Stick (NCS). ... The Raspberry Pi 3B+ combined with the NCS1 execution model exhibits a latency of 0.25s and power consumption of 6W, and the Raspberry Pi 3B+ combined with the NCS2 execution model demonstrates a latency of 0.2s and power consumption of 5.6W.","value_as_printed":"RPi 3B++NCS1: 0.25s, 6W; RPi 3B++NCS2: 0.2s, 5.6W; f_c=2GHz (LoS); P_v=30 dBm; B_c^mm=28GHz; B^max=20 MHz; sigma^2=-174 dBm; P^{v,hov}=60 w","units":"s, W, GHz, dBm, MHz","baseline":null,"workload":"YOLOv3-tiny classifier execution on UAV","conditions":"UAV-UAV LoS link (§IV-B-1 p. 5); UAV-BS mmWave link (§IV-B-2 p. 5, P^{v->b}=30 dBm); Table II simulation parameters (p. 9)","rationale":"Every hardware, radio, noise, bandwidth, and hovering-power value and unit matches §VII-A-2 and Table II on p. 9 (with link models in §IV-B p. 5)."}
{"report_id":"R0048","evidence_key":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery:1","status":"PARTIAL","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","physical_pages":[5,6,9,12],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§IV-A, §V-A Eq. (18), Table II, §VIII Conclusion","excerpt_or_cell":"In future work, we will consider UAV trajectory planning, mobility constraints (e.g., minimum turning radius and climb rate), and interference from other UAVs.","value_as_printed":"h=200m; d_min=10 m; Gamma_LoS=5dB; future work: trajectory planning, minimum turning radius, climb rate, interference from other UAVs","units":"m, dB","baseline":null,"workload":"Hovering multi-UAV surveillance","conditions":"Table II (p. 9) gives h=200m, d_min=10 m, Gamma_LoS=5dB; §VIII (p. 12) states future work items","rationale":"Supported for hovering at 200 m, d_min=10 m, LoS attenuation 5 dB (Table II p. 9), and deferring trajectory planning and turning/climb constraints to future work (§VIII p. 12). PARTIAL because the clause 'and failures deferred to future work' is not supported: §VIII (p. 12) defers 'interference from other UAVs' and DRL for dynamic scenarios, not failures."}
{"report_id":"R0048","evidence_key":"evaluation_platform_simulator_hardware_and_traces:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","physical_pages":[9],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§VII, §VII-A-1, §VII-A-2","excerpt_or_cell":"The simulations are implemented using PyTorch 2.3.0 with Python 3.8.19 on an Intel Core i7-13700KF CPU server with two NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 24GB GPUs. ... We adopt OpenCV 3.2.0 for real-time video processing. ... We employ the CVXPY toolkit to address the communication resource allocation problem.","value_as_printed":"Python 3.8.19, PyTorch 2.3.0, OpenCV 3.2.0, CVXPY; Intel Core i7-13700KF CPU + 2x NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 24GB GPUs; RPi 3B++NCS1/NCS2 latency/power measured in advance [41]","units":"GB, s, W","baseline":null,"workload":"YOLOv3-tiny video analytics simulation","conditions":"Pre-measured execution latency/power for RPi 3B++NCS1 (0.25s, 6W) and NCS2 (0.2s, 5.6W) citing [41]","rationale":"All listed software versions, server CPU/GPU specs, CVXPY toolkit, and pre-measured RPi 3B++NCS1/NCS2 latency/power values are explicitly stated on physical p. 9."}
{"report_id":"R0048","evidence_key":"baselines_and_workloads:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","physical_pages":[9,10],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§VII-A-3","excerpt_or_cell":"We compare JDTSO and MAPDP against five fundamental benchmark methods. • Centralized Global Optimization Based on PPO (C-PPO) [46] ... • Centralized Global Optimization Based on DQN (C-DQN) [47] ... • Multi-agent deep deterministic policy gradient (MADDPG) [48] ... • Random ... • IND-UAV [9]","value_as_printed":"C-PPO, C-DQN, MADDPG, Random, IND-UAV","units":null,"baseline":"C-PPO, C-DQN, MADDPG, Random, IND-UAV","workload":"Real-time video analytics ROIs (YOLOv3-tiny DAG)","conditions":"Evaluated across 5-200 UAVs in §VII-B (Figs. 3-8)","rationale":"§VII-A-3 (pp. 9-10) explicitly enumerates all five baselines: C-PPO, C-DQN, MADDPG, Random, and IND-UAV."}
{"report_id":"R0048","evidence_key":"outcomes_latency_energy_communication_accuracy_reliability_and_scalability:1","status":"SUPPORTED","pdf_relative_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0048.pdf","physical_pages":[10,11,12],"section_or_table_or_figure":"§VII-B, Figs. 3-8, §VII-C, Table III","excerpt_or_cell":"The JDTSO algorithm achieved the lowest inference latency when the UAV's number is 20 or fewer. ... As the number of UAVs increased, this algorithm not find a suitable solution within a realistic calculation time using this algorithm. However, the MAPDP algorithm can provide better solutions for larger UAV scales. ... Table III presents the sensitivity analysis results for the energy efficiency with 60 UAVs.","value_as_printed":"Table III (60 UAVs): phi_v=0.6 (0.515 s, 778.7 J, 116.6 IPS), phi_v=0.7 (0.511 s, 670.8 J, 117.4 IPS), phi_v=0.8 (0.513 s, 596.1 J, 117.1 IPS)","units":"s, J, IPS","baseline":"C-PPO, C-DQN, MADDPG, Random, IND-UAV","workload":"YOLOv3-tiny branching DAG ROIs","conditions":"JDTSO <=20 UAVs (no feasible runtime >20); MAPDP 40-200 UAVs (Figs. 3-8); Table III at V=60 UAVs across phi_v in {0.6, 0.7, 0.8}","rationale":"§VII-B (pp. 10-11) and Figs. 3-8 confirm JDTSO achieves lowest latency/energy and highest throughput for <=20 UAVs (failing to find a solution in realistic time >20) while MAPDP outperforms all baselines for 40-200 UAVs; Table III (p. 12) exact cells match the 60-UAV ranges 0.511-0.515 s, 596.1-778.7 J, and 116.6-117.4 IPS."}
